\title{The Geometry of a Knee Distribution:\\ Fermat--Weber Centres, Projections, and Scaling Rays}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop the one-dimensional Fermat--Weber (geometric median) theory needed to analyse a
family of empirical \emph{knee distributions}, and use it to convert informal empirical claims
into sharp, falsifiable geometric statements. The empirical setting is a threshold search:
at each context length $\ctx$ one measures the smallest budget $k^{*}$ at which a quality metric
still clears a fixed bar. Three repetitions at $\ctx=1024$ gave $\{96,112,128\}$; three at
$\ctx=2048$ gave $\{160,224,256\}$. With $P=d\cdot\ctx/32$ the natural product point ($128$ and
$256$), both medians equal $\tfrac78 P$ while individual measurements scatter.
We prove: (i) a complete characterisation of Fermat--Weber points on a line --- a point minimises
total distance iff it is \emph{balanced} --- with sufficiency in any linearly ordered abelian
group and a finitary necessity proof over $\R$; (ii) a sharp slope bound
$F(m)+|t-m|\le F(t)$ for odd samples, from which uniqueness follows, together with its
metric-space form; (iii) that the median of three, with two arguments fixed, is the
nearest-point projection onto the segment they span --- monotone, firmly nonexpansive, with
half-line fibres at the endpoints; (iv) that the top edge and the median lie on rays through the
origin of slopes $1/8$ and $7/64=\tfrac78\cdot\tfrac18$ while the low tail lies on none
(determinant $-32768$), with dilation defect exactly $P/8$, and normalised optimal costs $1/4$
and $3/8$ in exact ratio $3/2$; (v) that both normalised measurements lie on a maximal flat face
of a non-convex level set of the median map; and (vi) a pre-registered prediction for a pending
fourth measurement: the $7/8$ centre remains optimal for every possible new value, the optimal
cost is exactly $96+|224-x|$, and the optimum becomes a segment with one endpoint pinned at the
centre.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Fermat--Weber point, geometric median, metric projection, firm
nonexpansiveness, order statistics, dilation equivariance, level sets, knee distribution.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The empirical setting}

Consider an experiment parameterised by a depth $d$ and a context length $\ctx$. For a budget
$k$ one measures a quality ratio $q(k)\in[0,1]$ --- performance retained at budget $k$ relative
to full budget --- and defines the \emph{knee}
\[
k^{*} \;=\; \min\{k \text{ in the sweep grid} : q(k)\ge\beta\}
\]
for a fixed bar $\beta$ (here $\beta=0.98$). Two structural constants organise the data. The
first is the \emph{product point} $P=d\cdot\ctx/32$, equal to $128$ at $(d,\ctx)=(4,1024)$ and
$256$ at $(4,2048)$; empirically $k^{*}\le P$ at every measurement, at both contexts. The second
is the ratio $7/8$, which appears not in any single measurement but in the \emph{centre} of the
measured distribution.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
context & knees $k^{*}$ & product point $P$ & ratios $k^{*}/P$ & median\\
\midrule
$1024$ & $\{96,112,128\}$ & $128$ & $\{3/4,\,7/8,\,1\}$ & $112=\tfrac78\cdot 128$\\
$2048$ & $\{160,224,256\}$ & $256$ & $\{5/8,\,7/8,\,1\}$ & $224=\tfrac78\cdot 256$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The most recent measurement (third repetition at $\ctx=2048$) returned $k^{*}=160$, crossing the
bar with margin $+0.0012$ --- the tightest of the series, indicating a true threshold near
$150$--$160$ falling between grid points. Four point predictions recorded in advance ($224$,
$240$, $256$, $192$) were all refuted. The distributional claim, that the median sits at
$\tfrac78 P$, was not.

\subsection{Why geometry}

On a line the median coincides with the \emph{Fermat--Weber point}, the minimiser of total
distance to the sample. Read variationally, the median yields a quantitative stability bound, a
characterisation valid at every sample size and parity, and a scaling analysis in which
Fermat--Weber cost is a genuine transport cost comparable across contexts. All three are needed
to state the pending experiment's prediction correctly.

\subsection{Notation}

Samples are finite multisets. For a multiset $S$ in a linearly ordered abelian group and a
candidate centre $t$, the \emph{Fermat--Weber cost} is $F_S(t)=\sum_{x\in S}|t-x|$, counted with
multiplicity. We write $a\wedge b=\min(a,b)$, $a\vee b=\max(a,b)$, and
$\med(a,b,c)=(a\wedge b)\vee(b\wedge c)\vee(a\wedge c)$. For a sample of odd size $2k+1$, $m$ is a
\emph{counting median} if at least $k+1$ entries are $\le m$ and at least $k+1$ are $\ge m$.

\section{Fermat--Weber points of a triple: the metric-space core}

\begin{definition}[Metric betweenness]
In a metric space $(X,d)$, a point $q$ lies \emph{metrically between} $p$ and $r$ if
$d(p,q)+d(q,r)=d(p,r)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Triple lower bound]\label{thm:triple}
For all $p,q,r,x$ in a metric space,
$d(p,r)+d(x,q)\le d(x,p)+d(x,q)+d(x,r)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The triangle inequality gives $d(p,r)\le d(x,p)+d(x,r)$; add $d(x,q)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Betweenness points are geometric medians]\label{thm:between}
If $q$ lies metrically between $p$ and $r$, then for every $x$,
\[
d(x,p)+d(x,q)+d(x,r)\;\ge\;d(p,r),
\]
with equality if and only if $x=q$. The optimal value is the diameter $d(p,r)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At $x=q$ the sum is $d(q,p)+0+d(q,r)=d(p,r)$ by betweenness. In general
Theorem~\ref{thm:triple} bounds the sum below by $d(p,r)+d(x,q)$, so it is at least $d(p,r)$,
with equality forcing $d(x,q)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The real line]\label{cor:real}
For $a\le b\le c$ and $x\in\R$,
$|x-a|+|x-b|+|x-c|\ge c-a$, with equality iff $x=b=\med(a,b,c)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Sortedness gives $|a-b|+|b-c|=c-a=|a-c|$, i.e.\ $b$ is metrically between $a$ and $c$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:between}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Collinear data in a normed space]\label{cor:line}
Let $E$ be a real normed space, $v\in E$, $\|u\|=1$, and $a\le b\le c$. Then for every $x\in E$,
\[
d(x,v+au)+d(x,v+bu)+d(x,v+cu)\;\ge\;c-a,
\]
with equality iff $x=v+bu$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$d(v+su,v+tu)=|s-t|$, so $v+bu$ is metrically between the outer points; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:between}.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:line} states uniqueness \emph{in the ambient space}: no off-line point does
better.

\begin{application}[The measured triples]
By Corollary~\ref{cor:real}: for all $x$, $|x-160|+|x-224|+|x-256|\ge 96$ with equality iff
$x=224=\tfrac78\cdot 256$; and $|x-96|+|x-112|+|x-128|\ge 32$ with equality iff
$x=112=\tfrac78\cdot 128$. The ``$7/8$ median law'' is thus a variational statement.
\end{application}

\section{The general characterisation: balanced points}

\begin{definition}[Balance]
Let $S$ be a finite multiset in a linearly ordered abelian group and $m$ a point. Say $m$ is
\emph{balanced} for $S$ if
\[
\#\{x\in S: x>m\}\le\#\{x\in S: x\le m\}
\quad\text{and}\quad
\#\{x\in S: x<m\}\le\#\{x\in S: x\ge m\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Two-valued sums]\label{lem:twoval}
For a finite multiset $S$, a decidable predicate $p$ and constants $c,d$ in an abelian group,
\[
\sum_{x\in S}\bigl(c \text{ if } p(x),\ d \text{ otherwise}\bigr)
= \#\{x: p(x)\}\cdot c + \#\{x:\neg p(x)\}\cdot d .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sufficiency]\label{thm:suff}
If $m$ is balanced for $S$, then $F_S(m)\le F_S(t)$ for every $t$. This holds in any linearly
ordered abelian group.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Assume $m\le t$; set $A=\#\{x\le m\}$ and $C=\#\{x>m\}$, so $C\le A$. Pointwise,
\[
|m-x| + \begin{cases} t-m, & x\le m\\ -(t-m), & x>m\end{cases}\;\le\;|t-x|,
\]
with equality in the first case and the bound $-(t-x)\le|t-x|$ in the second. Summing and
applying Lemma~\ref{lem:twoval},
$F_S(m) + A\,(t-m) + C\,(-(t-m)) \le F_S(t)$. Writing $A=C+r$ with $r\ge 0$, the middle terms
collapse to $r\,(t-m)\ge 0$. The case $t\le m$ is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Odd samples are balanced at the counting median]
If $|S|=2k+1$ and $m$ is a counting median, then $m$ is balanced.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\#\{x\le m\}\ge k+1$ and $\#\{x\le m\}+\#\{x>m\}=2k+1$ give $\#\{x>m\}\le k$; symmetrically for
the other side.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp slope bound]\label{thm:slope}
Let $|S|=2k+1$ with counting median $m$. Then $F_S(m)+|t-m|\le F_S(t)$ for every $t$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
As in Theorem~\ref{thm:suff}, but now $C+1\le A$; writing $A=C+r+1$ the collapse leaves
$r\,(t-m)+(t-m)\ge t-m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniqueness in odd size]
For $|S|$ odd with counting median $m$, $F_S(m)<F_S(t)$ for all $t\ne m$, and every minimiser
equals $m$.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Necessity}

\begin{lemma}[Exact cost of a gap-free step]\label{lem:step}
Let $S\subset\R$ be finite and $m\le t$ with no sample point strictly between $m$ and $t$. Then
\[
F_S(t)=F_S(m)+\bigl(\#\{x\le m\}-\#\{x>m\}\bigr)(t-m),
\]
and symmetrically for a leftward gap-free step.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Under the gap hypothesis no absolute value changes sign, so $|t-x|=|m-x|\pm(t-m)$ according to
the side of $x$; sum and apply Lemma~\ref{lem:twoval}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Necessity]\label{thm:nec}
If $m\in\R$ minimises $F_S$, then $m$ is balanced for $S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $A=\#\{x\le m\}<C=\#\{x>m\}$. Then $C>0$; let $t$ be the least sample point exceeding
$m$. No sample point lies strictly between, so Lemma~\ref{lem:step} gives
$F_S(t)=F_S(m)+(A-C)(t-m)<F_S(m)$, contradicting minimality. The other condition is symmetric,
stepping to the greatest sample point below $m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Characterisation]
For a finite multiset $S\subset\R$ and $m\in\R$: $m$ minimises $F_S$ if and only if $m$ is
balanced for $S$.
\end{theorem}

The witness in Theorem~\ref{thm:nec} is a data point, so the argument is finitary: no limiting
or derivative argument is used.

\subsection{Convexity and the shape of the optimal set}

\begin{theorem}[Convexity]\label{thm:convex}
For finite $S\subset\R$, $a,b\in\R$ and $\lambda\in[0,1]$,
$F_S(\lambda a+(1-\lambda)b)\le\lambda F_S(a)+(1-\lambda)F_S(b)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Pointwise, $|\lambda(a-x)+(1-\lambda)(b-x)|\le\lambda|a-x|+(1-\lambda)|b-x|$; sum over $S$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The optimal set is an interval]
If $a$ and $b$ both minimise $F_S$ and $a\le t\le b$, then $t$ minimises $F_S$. Hence the
Fermat--Weber set is a closed interval.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $t=\lambda a+(1-\lambda)b$. Minimality gives $F_S(a)=F_S(b)$, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:convex} yields $F_S(t)\le F_S(b)\le F_S(u)$ for all $u$.
\end{proof}

\section{Even samples: the optimum becomes a segment}

\begin{theorem}[Four-point Fermat--Weber]\label{thm:four}
Let $a\le b\le c\le d$ and $C_4(t)=|t-a|+|t-b|+|t-c|+|t-d|$. Then
$C_4(t)\ge(d-a)+(c-b)$ for every $t$, with equality if and only if $b\le t\le c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The bound sums $|t-a|+|t-d|\ge d-a$ and $|t-b|+|t-c|\ge c-b$. If $b\le t\le c$, expanding the
four absolute values with their determined signs gives exactly $(d-a)+(c-b)$. If $t<b$, then
$|t-b|+|t-c|=b+c-2t>c-b$, so the total strictly exceeds the bound; $t>c$ is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The Fermat--Weber set of a sorted four-point sample is the middle segment $[b,c]$; it is a single
point iff $b=c$.
\end{corollary}

For an even sample, ``the median'' is a set, not a number. A point prediction about the median of
an even-sized experiment is therefore already the wrong type of object.

\section{The median of three as a metric projection}

\begin{definition}[Clamp]
For $a,b,x$ in a linear order, $\cl_{a,b}(x)=(a\wedge b)\vee\bigl((a\vee b)\wedge x\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Median $=$ clamp]
$\med(a,b,x)=\cl_{a,b}(x)$ for all $a,b,x$ in a linear order.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both sides are lattice polynomials; case analysis on the relative order of $a,b,x$ evaluates both
to the middle element.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nearest-point projection]\label{thm:proj}
Let $a\le b$ in $\R$. Then $\cl_{a,b}$ maps $\R$ into $[a,b]$, is the identity on $[a,b]$, and for
every $y\in[a,b]$ satisfies $|\cl_{a,b}(x)-x|\le|y-x|$, with equality only for
$y=\cl_{a,b}(x)$. Thus $\cl_{a,b}$ is the unique nearest-point projection onto $[a,b]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Three cases: $x<a$ gives clamp $a$ and $a-x\le y-x$; $x>b$ is symmetric; $x\in[a,b]$ gives clamp
$x$ at distance $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Regularity]\label{thm:reg}
For fixed $a\le b$, write $P=\cl_{a,b}$. Then $P$ is monotone; nonexpansive,
$|P(x)-P(y)|\le|x-y|$; and firmly nonexpansive,
\[
\bigl(P(x)-P(y)\bigr)^{2}\;\le\;(x-y)\bigl(P(x)-P(y)\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Monotonicity is case analysis. For $x\le y$, monotonicity gives $0\le P(y)-P(x)$ and
nonexpansiveness gives $P(y)-P(x)\le y-x$; multiplying these two inequalities gives the firm
bound, which is symmetric in $x,y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fibres]\label{thm:fibres}
Let $a<b$. Then $\cl_{a,b}(x)=a\iff x\le a$; $\cl_{a,b}(x)=b\iff b\le x$; and for $a<m<b$,
$\cl_{a,b}(x)=m\iff x=m$.
\end{theorem}

Fibres over the endpoints are closed half-lines --- the normal cones of the segment --- while
interior fibres are singletons. Stability of the centre is a boundary phenomenon.

\begin{theorem}[Range]
For $a\le b$, $\{\med(a,b,x):x\in\R\}=[a,b]$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Consequences for the measured data}

At $\ctx=2048$ the two earlier measurements are $256$ and $224$, spanning $[224,256]$.

\begin{proposition}[Stability ray]
$\med(256,224,x)=224$ if and only if $x\le 224$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[A plausible informal claim is false]
The assertion ``only a third measurement $\ge 256$ moves the centre off $224$'' fails:
$\med(256,224,240)=240\ne 224$ with $240<256$. The correct threshold is the near endpoint $224$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[The excursion is absorbed]
$|\cl_{224,256}(160)-160|=64$, and $|y-160|\ge 64$ for every $y\in[224,256]$: the measurement sits
$64$ units below the segment and the projection absorbs the entire excursion.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Contrast with the mean]
With two arguments fixed, $x\mapsto(a+b+x)/3$ is a surjection onto $\R$. On the measured triple
its value is $640/3\approx 213.33\ne 224$, whereas the median is $224=\tfrac78\cdot 256$; the
median's range is the compact segment $[224,256]$.
\end{proposition}

\section{Scaling geometry: rays, dilations, and one scalar defect}

Plot each measurement as $(\ctx,k^{*})\in\R^{2}$. For $p,q\in\R^{2}$ write
$p\times q=p_1q_2-p_2q_1$; two points lie on a common ray through the origin iff $p\times q=0$,
and the origin triangle has area $|p\times q|/2$.

\begin{theorem}[The top edge is a ray]
$(1024,128)\times(2048,256)=0$, both of slope $1/8$: the product-point law is exact
proportionality.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The median is a ray]
$(1024,112)\times(2048,224)=0$, both of slope $7/64=\tfrac78\cdot\tfrac18$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness and predictive content of the median slope]
If $s\in\R$ satisfies $112=s\cdot 1024$, then $s=7/64=\tfrac78\cdot\tfrac18$ and moreover
$224=s\cdot 2048$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The hypothesis determines $s=112/1024=7/64$; then $\tfrac{7}{64}\cdot 2048=224$.
\end{proof}

One context determines the constant with no remaining freedom; the other tests it.

\begin{theorem}[The low tail is not a ray]
$(1024,96)\times(2048,160)=-32768\ne 0$, and the origin triangle has area $16384$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Dilation equivariance and its failure]
The doubling dilation $\delta_2(p)=2p$ carries $(1024,128)\mapsto(2048,256)$ and
$(1024,112)\mapsto(2048,224)$, but $(1024,96)\mapsto(2048,192)\ne(2048,160)$, with defect
$2\cdot 96-160=32=256/8=P/8$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[No dilation matches the whole configuration]
There is no $t\in\R$ with $t\,(1024,128)=(2048,256)$, $t\,(1024,112)=(2048,224)$ and
$t\,(1024,96)=(2048,160)$ simultaneously.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The first forces $t=2$; the third then demands $2\cdot 96=160$, which is false.
\end{proof}

Thus the knee \emph{distribution} is not self-similar under context doubling, though its centre
and upper edge are; all the non-self-similarity is concentrated in one scalar, $P/8$.

\subsection{The spread, exactly}

Normalise by the product point: $r_8=(3/4,\,7/8,\,1)$ and $r_{16}=(5/8,\,7/8,\,1)$.

\begin{theorem}[Normalised optimal costs]
For all $x$, $|x-\tfrac34|+|x-\tfrac78|+|x-1|\ge\tfrac14$ with equality iff $x=\tfrac78$, and
$|x-\tfrac58|+|x-\tfrac78|+|x-1|\ge\tfrac38$ with equality iff $x=\tfrac78$. Hence the optimal
costs are $1/4$ and $3/8$, and $\tfrac38=\tfrac32\cdot\tfrac14$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The widening is carried entirely by the low tail]
The top two normalised coordinates agree at the two contexts, and
$\tfrac38-\tfrac14=\tfrac18=\tfrac34-\tfrac58$.
\end{theorem}

\section{A flat face inside a non-convex level set}

Regard normalised triples as points of $\R^{3}$ and let
$\mathcal{L}=\med^{-1}(7/8)\subset\R^{3}$.

\begin{theorem}[A maximal flat edge]\label{thm:edge}
For every $t\le 7/8$, $\med(t,\tfrac78,1)=\tfrac78$; and for $\tfrac78<t\le 1$,
$\med(t,\tfrac78,1)=t$. So the half-line $H=\{(t,\tfrac78,1):t\le\tfrac78\}$ lies in
$\mathcal{L}$ and is maximal in its direction.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Both contexts lie on the edge]
$r_8,r_{16}\in H$, and for every $s\le 1$,
$\med\bigl(s\,r_8+(1-s)\,r_{16}\bigr)=\tfrac78$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The second and third coordinates of the combination are $\tfrac78$ and $1$; the first is
$\tfrac58+\tfrac{s}{8}\le\tfrac78$ for $s\le 2$. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:edge}.
\end{proof}

Only $s\le 1$ is needed, so the flat face extends past the $16\times$ endpoint: the low tail may
keep decreasing over further contexts without moving the centre.

\begin{theorem}[The level set is not convex]
$\med(\tfrac58,\tfrac78,1)=\tfrac78$ and $\med(\tfrac78,1,\tfrac58)=\tfrac78$, but their midpoint
$(\tfrac34,\tfrac{15}{16},\tfrac{13}{16})$ has median $\tfrac{13}{16}\ne\tfrac78$.
\end{theorem}

Hence the previous theorem has genuine content: two points can each have the right centre while
the segment joining them leaves the level set.

\section{The pending fourth measurement: a pre-registered prediction}

Write $C(x,t)=|t-160|+|t-224|+|t-256|+|t-x|$ for a fourth value $x$ and candidate centre $t$.

\begin{theorem}[Value at the centre]
$C(x,224)=96+|224-x|$ for every $x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The centre is optimal for every fourth value]
$C(x,224)\le C(x,t)$ for all $x,t\in\R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$|t-160|+|t-256|\ge 96$ and $|t-224|+|t-x|\ge|224-x|$; add.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact linear cost response]
The optimal four-value cost is exactly $96+|224-x|$: the response to the new datum is linear with
slope $1$ away from $224$. What a fourth measurement moves is the spread, never the centre.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Regimes]
For $160\le x\le 224$: $C(x,t)=(256-160)+(224-x)$ iff $x\le t\le 224$. For $224\le x\le 256$:
$C(x,t)=(256-160)+(x-224)$ iff $224\le t\le x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:four} with sorted data $(160,x,224,256)$ and $(160,224,x,256)$ respectively.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Knife edge for uniqueness]
For $160\le x\le 224$, the centre $224$ is the unique optimum iff $x=224$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Balance form]
For every $y\in\R$ the point $224$ is balanced for $\{160,224,256,y\}$: if $y\le 224$ then three
of four entries are $\le 224$ and two are $\ge 224$; otherwise two lie on each side. Sufficiency
then gives optimality.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The prediction is not refutable by the pending measurement, and this is informative rather than
embarrassing: the geometry guarantees the centre survives, so the next measurement tests the
\emph{low tail}. In variational language, the outcome to watch is the lower endpoint of the
optimal segment.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{description}
\item[A. Fermat--Weber set on a line.] Sort in $O(n\log n)$ (or select in $O(n)$); return
$[x_{(k+1)},x_{(k+1)}]$ if $n=2k+1$ and $[x_{(k)},x_{(k+1)}]$ if $n=2k$. The optimal cost is the
sum of nested spreads $\sum_{i\le\lfloor n/2\rfloor}(x_{(n+1-i)}-x_{(i)})$.
\item[B. Balance test.] One pass, $O(n)$: report balanced iff
$n-\#\{x\le m\}\le\#\{x\le m\}$ and $\#\{x<m\}\le n-\#\{x<m\}$. This is an exact optimality
certificate; no cost evaluation is needed.
\item[C. Stability radius of the centre.] By the fibre theorem, the set of values of a free
coordinate leaving the centre unchanged is a half-line when the centre sits at an endpoint of the
segment spanned by the fixed data, and a singleton otherwise. For the measured data it is
$(-\infty,224]$.
\item[D. Ray and dilation diagnostics.] For paired measurements, the determinant certifies (or
quantifies departure from) a proportional law; for a doubling pair, $2y_1-y_2$ measures the
failure of dilation equivariance.
\end{description}

\section{Discussion}

Three informal claims were adjudicated. First, ``the median is the robust quantity'': true and
quantitatively so, since the median of three is a metric projection, firmly nonexpansive with an
unbounded stability half-line at each endpoint, whereas the mean is surjective in the free
coordinate. Second, ``only a third measurement $\ge 256$ would move the centre'': false, since
$\med(256,224,240)=240$; the threshold is the near endpoint $224$. Third, ``the longer context's
spread is about $50\%$ wider'': exactly right, and exactly $3/2$, with the widening carried wholly
by the low coordinate.

The round separates point predictions from structural ones. Four point predictions about a single
measurement failed; one structural prediction about the centre held. The asymmetry is not luck: a
single measurement is a grid-quantised crossing of a noisy curve (margin $+0.0012$, true threshold
near $150$--$160$), while the median is the image of a firmly nonexpansive projection whose fibre
over the reported value is an entire half-line.

Honest limits: the $7/8$ law rests on two contexts, so its confirmation is one independent test,
not many; the low-tail ratio $5/8$ rests on a single measurement, so the defect $P/8$ is an exact
statement about one measured pair rather than a verified law; and the pending fourth measurement
cannot refute the centre, which bounds what it can tell us.

Two results are stated more generally than the data requires: the metric-space betweenness theorem
holds in any metric space, and the normed-space corollary shows collinear data have their
geometric median at the middle point \emph{in the ambient space}. Likewise sufficiency of balance
holds in any linearly ordered abelian group; only necessity uses the reals, and then only through
the existence of a nearest datum on each side, so it applies unchanged to integer grids.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Fourth-value dichotomy]
For the pending fourth value $x$, the Fermat--Weber optimum of $\{160,224,256,x\}$ is the segment
with endpoints $\min(x,224)\vee 160$ and $\max(x,224)\wedge 256$, whose endpoint at $224$ is pinned
for every $x$, with optimal cost $96+|224-x|$. Hence no fourth value can refute the $7/8$ centre
variationally; it can only destroy uniqueness, and does so unless $x=224$. The pinning, the cost
law, the regimes and the knife edge are established above; what remains conjectural is the
empirical claim that the measured value lands in $[160,256]$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[The low-tail defect grows linearly in the context]
Writing $L(\ctx)$ for the smallest three-measurement knee, the points $(\ctx,L(\ctx))$ are not
collinear with the origin, and $2L(\ctx)-L(2\,\ctx)=P(2\,\ctx)/8$. The measured pair gives
$2\cdot 96-160=32=256/8$. A third context $\ctx=4096$ predicts low tail
$2\cdot 160-512/8=256$, ratio $1/2$, cleanly separated from the equivariant alternative $5/8$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Median stability as firm nonexpansiveness, with a sharp constant]
For any statistic $S:\R^{n}\to\R$ that is symmetric, translation equivariant, monotone, and firmly
nonexpansive in each coordinate with the others fixed, the stability set of a reported value is a
closed interval, unbounded precisely when the reported value is an endpoint of the range of $S$ in
that coordinate. The median satisfies all four hypotheses with sharp constant $1$; the mean fails
the fourth in the required uniform sense and has singleton stability sets.
\end{conjecture}

Further measurable cells: a fourth repetition at $\ctx=1024$ (low information, the centre there
being pinned by the same projection argument); a depth-$8$ corner at short context, to test whether
the constant $7/8$ is depth independent; and a compression-floor check at depth $8$.

\section{Conclusion}

The median on a line is the Fermat--Weber point, and we characterised those points completely: a
point is optimal exactly when it is balanced, with sufficiency in any linearly ordered abelian
group and a finitary necessity proof over the reals. The optimal set is always an interval --- a
point in odd size, a segment in even size. Holding two measurements fixed, the median of three is
the nearest-point projection onto the segment they span, monotone and firmly nonexpansive, with
half-line fibres at the endpoints; this is the mechanism of robustness, and it is quantitative
enough to explain why a $64$-unit excursion moved the reported centre by nothing.

Across contexts the picture is two rays and a defect: the top edge and the median are exactly
proportional to the context with slopes $1/8$ and $7/64$, the low tail is not proportional at all,
and the failure of dilation equivariance is the single scalar $P/8$. The spread grows by exactly
$3/2$ per doubling, entirely through the low coordinate, and both normalised measurements lie on a
maximal flat face of a non-convex level set of the median map. Finally, the geometry issues a
prediction about the pending fourth measurement that cannot fail --- which is precisely how one
learns that the next experiment tests the low tail rather than the centre.

\end{document}

